//? Title: Rare-event g and t charts
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Two synthetic event logs track the same kind of process in different units.
The g-chart records the count of event-free opportunities between events. The
t-chart records positive time intervals. Eight initial gaps fit each baseline,
then the logs contain an unusually long gap followed by closely spaced events.
)

> Fit, monitor and plot

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/rare_event_charts.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>


namespace spc = datamunge::stats;
using datamunge::graphics::RLayout;

void show_signals(const spc::ControlChart& chart) {
    for (const auto& p : chart.points)
        if (p.outside)
            std::print("| {} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |\n",
                       chart.title, p.index, p.value, p.lower, p.upper);
}

int main() {
    const std::vector<std::size_t> between{12, 20, 18, 9, 25, 13, 17, 24, 16, 145, 0, 18};
    const std::vector<double> hours{5, 7, 6, 4, 8, 3, 6, 5, 7, 48, 0.001, 6};
    const spc::ControlChartOptions baseline{.baseline_size = 8};
    const auto g = spc::g_chart(between, baseline);
    const auto t = spc::t_chart(hours, baseline);
    std::print("| Chart | Gap | Statistic | Lower | Upper |\n"
               "| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    show_signals(g);
    show_signals(t);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    RLayout::create(1, 2).size(1100, 400).add(g.plot()).add(t.plot())
        .save_svg("build/rare_event_charts.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Chart | Gap | Statistic | Lower | Upper |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| g-chart | 10 | 145.000 | 0.000 | 117.000 |
| t-chart | 10 | 48.000 | 0.007 | 36.342 |
| t-chart | 11 | 0.001 | 0.007 | 36.342 |
\image(figures/rare_event_charts.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Observed gaps against geometric and exponential limits fitted from the first eight intervals.)
\alttext(The discrete gap chart flags a long gap. The continuous gap chart displays both very long and very short intervals against its limits.)

> Interpret the chart

Each center line is the fitted median. The limits use the fitted geometric
or exponential tails, rather than a symmetric band around the center. The
integer lower limit can land at zero, so a zero gap on this g-chart is on the
limit and does not signal under the strict outside-limit rule. The continuous
t-chart can flag unusually small positive gaps.

> References

- [Benneyan, Number-between g-type statistical quality control charts|https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1011846412909]
